;; manifest/orgs-detectors.edn — the registry of detectors that CANNOT be fleet
;; gates, and therefore run on this machine instead.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; ADR-2608124800 measured that roughly twenty verification scripts in this
;; workspace cannot be murakumo fleet gates, for a structural reason CLAUDE.md
;; already records: **a fleet gate ships only the target repo's own tree**, so a
;; detector that reads `orgs/` finds an empty workspace on a node and can never
;; go green there. `root-permit-index` has been 0 pass / 282 fail for exactly
;; this reason. Two of the detectors say so in their own headers.
;;
;; So the answer for them is not a `gates.edn` line. It is a home on a machine
;; that actually has `orgs/`. `scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` is that home and
;; this file is what it runs.
;;
;; ## What an entry must declare, and why each field is mandatory
;;
;;   :id            keyword, stable. Findings are keyed under it forever.
;;   :title         one line, shown to a human at session start.
;;   :why           what it would have caught, with a date. An entry that cannot
;;                  answer this is a detector nobody has justified running.
;;   :argv          the command. `{{repo}}` is substituted with the checkout
;;                  holding the tick script, `{{root}}` with the orgs-bearing
;;                  checkout. They differ when the tick runs from a worktree.
;;   :cwd           :root (the orgs-bearing checkout) or :repo.
;;   :findings      :protocol -- the detector prints FINDING<TAB>sev<TAB>key<TAB>detail
;;                  lines. This is the only supported mode, deliberately: a tick
;;                  that regex-scraped prose reports would mis-key findings the
;;                  first time a message was reworded, and silently report every
;;                  standing finding as new.
;;   :evidence      regex that MUST appear in the output for the run to be
;;                  recorded :ok. This is the anti-theatre floor. ADR-2608124800
;;                  found five scripts that print PASSED without measuring; a
;;                  detector that dies after two lines must be recorded
;;                  :inconclusive, never clean. `SCANNED\t0\t...` fails it too.
;;   :interval-ms   minimum age before re-running. Per detector, because the
;;                  measured runtimes differ by 5x.
;;   :timeout-ms    hard kill. No entry may omit it.
;;   :writes        MUST be :none. The tick refuses to run an entry that does
;;                  not declare it. See the invariants below.
;;   :measured      what was actually observed when the entry was added, so the
;;                  interval and timeout are traceable to a measurement rather
;;                  than to a guess.
;;   :accepted      OPTIONAL. finding-key -> {:since :why :clears-when}, all
;;                  three non-empty strings, enforced by admission. A finding
;;                  listed here is CORRECT and deliberately not being fixed yet;
;;                  the tick reports it as ACCEPTED, never as NEW, and never
;;                  inside the STANDING count.
;;
;;                  It exists because the 2026-08-13 triage (ADR-2608132600)
;;                  needed a state the three classes could not express. Without
;;                  it, "we looked at this, we understand it, and the fix is a
;;                  decision someone else has to make" decays into STANDING --
;;                  the class this whole design calls indistinguishable from
;;                  silence. The decision would then be recorded nowhere, and a
;;                  month later would read exactly like a defect nobody opened.
;;
;;                  Two things keep it from becoming a way to make reds go away.
;;                  (1) Admission rejects an entry with no date, no reason or no
;;                  exit condition, so accepting costs as much writing as fixing
;;                  a small thing. (2) An acceptance whose finding has gone away
;;                  is reported LOUDLY as a stale acceptance, in the tick log and
;;                  at session start -- an exemption that outlives its subject
;;                  exempts whatever lands on that key next.
;;
;; ## Invariants this home holds itself to
;;
;;   1. It reads `orgs/` checkouts. It NEVER writes to one, never fetches, never
;;      checks out, never moves HEAD anywhere. Detectors are admitted on
;;      `:writes :none`, reviewed by a human before being added here, and are
;;      run with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none` so any `git fetch`/`clone`/`push`
;;      inside a detector fails at the transport layer rather than succeeding
;;      quietly. See the tick's header for what that env var does and does not
;;      cover, stated honestly.
;;   2. It writes NOTHING into any git checkout, including the superproject.
;;      State lives under `~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/`. A tick that dirtied the
;;      shared checkout would trip the stash-churn failure CLAUDE.md documents.
;;   3. It is a monitor, not a gate: exit 0 even with findings. The gate is the
;;      individual detector, run by a human or by a hook.
;;
;; ## What this home cannot host, stated rather than implied
;;
;;   - `annex-custody-verify` needs B2 credentials. It can run here (the keys
;;     are on this machine, not on a fleet node) but NOT under launchd: launchd
;;     cannot answer a Keychain unlock prompt, the wall recorded in
;;     ADR-2607178000. It belongs in the hand-run set, and putting it here on a
;;     schedule would produce a permanent :inconclusive -- another standing red.
;;   - Anything needing `gh` is rate-limited and shares one token with every
;;     concurrent agent session on this machine. Not scheduled here.
;;   - `audit-gftd-scripts` reads `$HOME/.gftd`, which is correct here and
;;     meaningless on a node -- but it is cheap and ADR-2608124800 ranks it as a
;;     SessionStart hook, which is a better fit than a 6-hour tick.
;;
;; ## The 2026-08-13 sweep of `scripts/verify-*.cljs`, and what it ruled out
;;
;; Thirteen scripts under `scripts/verify-*.cljs` mention `orgs/`. Four are
;; registered below. The other nine, with the class each falls in:
;;
;;   - `verify-duplicate-registrations` -- **`gh` class.** It calls
;;     `gh api repos/<slug>` once per candidate group to confirm two paths are
;;     one upstream by GitHub id. Rate-limited, one token shared with every
;;     concurrent agent session.
;;   - `verify-namespace-collisions` -- **not swept.** A sibling session was
;;     working on it on 2026-08-13; registering it concurrently would have
;;     pinned an argv that was being changed underneath.
;;   - `verify-repository-roles` -- **already hosted.** It is the fleet gate
;;     `root-repository-roles`, diagnosed and returned to green by
;;     ADR-2608124800. It needs no second home.
;;   - `verify-parity-probes` -- **already hosted, and it does not read
;;     `orgs/`.** It is the fleet gate `root-parity-probe-policy`; the string
;;     `orgs/` appears in it only inside a regex that matches the TEXT of a
;;     probe, and in the header prose. Its only input is
;;     `90-docs/maturity/engine-parity.datoms.edn`, which is in root's own tree.
;;   - `verify-no-babashka` -- **does not read `orgs/` either**, for the same
;;     reason: `--root orgs` appears in its usage comment, and its default root
;;     is `.`. ADR-2608124800 already classes it root-tree-only and gatable
;;     as-is, so what it is missing is a `gates.edn` line, not a registry entry.
;;     Measured here anyway, because pointing it at `orgs/` was the obvious
;;     thing to try: `--root orgs --allow-zero` ran for roughly 38 minutes on
;;     this machine without producing a line and was killed. It reads every file
;;     in the tree **in full** to test for a `bb` shebang, so its cost is
;;     dominated by the size of the artefacts it is not looking for.
;;   - `verify-clj-everywhere` -- **not a detector.** It is a test-suite
;;     orchestrator: four audits and then `clojure -M:test` in five repos. It
;;     emits no per-finding output to key on, it resolves Maven dependencies
;;     over HTTPS, and it writes to `~/.m2`, so `:writes :none` would be false.
;;   - `verify-kotoba-pages` -- **needs a real browser.** It drives Playwright
;;     against `file://` pages. CLAUDE.md's resource governor caps concurrent
;;     browser probes, and this machine's many parallel sessions already
;;     contend for OS focus; a browser launched unattended on a schedule is a
;;     materially different thing from a file-reading monitor.
;;   - `verify-vendored-copies`, `verify-require-graph` -- registered below
;;     since 2026-08-12.

{:schema 1

 :detectors
 [{:id :verify-wasm-toolchain-pin
   :title "the Wasm toolchain amu pins, against the versions the nodes actually have"
   :why "2026-08-20: amu's contract (kotoba-component's component-model-v1.edn,
              [:spec-baseline :wasi :toolchain]) pins {:wasm-tools \"1.243.0\"
              :wac-cli \"0.10.1\"} and refuses any other version before running.
              The fleet satisfies that from ~/.gftd/wasm-pin/bin, and the
              amu-jvm-test gate depends on it. When the contract moves, those
              binaries stay put and the gate goes red saying `wasm-tools version
              is not pinned` -- which reads as a repo failure and is not one. It
              is ADR-2608198600's shape (a node answering about itself, recorded
              as a verdict on the code), created by the change that added the
              gate. This says it in the right name, before the gate says it in
              the wrong one. Cannot be a fleet gate: the comparison needs
              kotoba-component's resource AND scripts/fleet-ci/nodes.edn, and no
              single shipped tree holds both. Measured while writing it -- the
              first version read the contract from amu, where it does not exist,
              and the refusal path is what caught that rather than a clean 0."
   :argv ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-wasm-toolchain-pin.cljs" "--findings" "--root" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd :root
   :findings :protocol
   :writes :none
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :timeout-ms 300000
   :interval-ms 43200000}
{:id       :verify-source-nul-bytes
   :title    "raw control bytes in source, which make grep answer as if a word is absent"
   :why      "2026-08-18/19: one NUL byte makes file(1) classify a source file as
              `data`, and grep then SKIPS it silently -- `grep -c MessageDigest
              <file>` prints nothing and exits 1, which is exactly what a file not
              containing that name does. Measured twice by different routes in one
              day: three searches of scripts/fleet-ci/tick.cljs came back empty and
              were read as `no such code`, and a portability scan reported `no JVM
              interop` for a file importing java.security.MessageDigest -- the same
              file having been copied into three repos. A sweep found 20 across the
              workspace, every byte load-bearing. This CANNOT be a fleet gate for
              orgs/: a gate ships one repo's tree and orgs/ is west-managed and
              absent from it. `root-source-nul-bytes` in gates.edn covers the
              superproject tree only, and says so."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-source-nul-bytes.cljs" "--findings" "{{root}}/orgs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 43200000                 ; 12h -- the tree is 144k files and the
                                         ; churn this catches is rare
   :timeout-ms  1800000                  ; 30m -- ~6x the measured 5m runtime
   :writes   :none}

  {:id       :verify-bridge-guest-cannot-execute
   :title    "the claude-bridge guest keeping the tools it is documented not to have"
   :why      "ADR-2608200200, 2026-08-20: the bridge's own header and the commit that
              shipped it both said a guest executing tools would be an agent running
              commands nobody sanctioned. The invariant was ASSERTED and never tested,
              and it was false -- `--allowedTools \"\"` is a PERMISSION allowlist, not an
              availability list, so all 25 built-ins stayed advertised and under
              permissionMode auto the guest ran Bash for real (`Run: echo
              hello-from-guest` -> tool_use Bash -> tool_result). The flag that removes
              availability is `--tools \"\"` (measured 25 -> 0). That fix landed and was
              checked by hand against a stub that was never committed, so nothing stops
              the next reader reaching the same wrong conclusion and deleting it. Static
              mode catches exactly that regression; --live is the only mode that proves
              the property, and it must probe with a value the model cannot produce
              without reading something -- the first attempt asked for `echo <marker>`,
              whose output is its own argument, and could not fail."
   :argv     ["nbb" "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-bridge-guest-cannot-execute.cljs"]
   :cwd      :repo
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  300000                   ; 5m -- far above the measured run, sized for load
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-20 on this machine: 0.17s at load 6.5, exit 0, `SCANNED\t1 bridge`,
              0 findings. It reads one config and spawns nothing.
              These four fields were missing when the entry landed the same day, and
              admission is all-or-nothing, so every detector was refused. That is the SECOND
              time this happened on 2026-08-20 -- :verify-error-provenance was the first --
              which is why `--admit-only` and the root-orgs-detectors-admission gate exist
              now. The rule was written down; what was missing was anything that checked it
              where an entry LANDS rather than when the tick next fires."}

  {:id       :verify-error-provenance
   :title    "errors classified from an exception that record none of it"
   :why      "ADR-2608198100, 2026-08-19: CLAUDE.md question 3 of five -- 受け取ったエラー本文を
              捨てていないか -- has been repo-wide mandatory since 2026-08-13 and was broken
              THREE TIMES in one day, twice in code written that day by someone who had read
              it. claude-bridge kept the HTTP status and dropped the body; parse-arguments
              kept the error type and dropped the arguments; record-turn! kept the error type
              and dropped the message. The third cost the most: 205 resident runs filed
              :internal-error, 196 of them saying \"request timed out\", readable only by
              walking 3,926 goal events -- and it hid a timeout fix that was already working.
              Prose did not prevent the third instance after two, which is the workspace's own
              argument for a hook rather than a sentence. Narrow by construction: it flags a
              map literal that READS (ex-data ...) and stores no message/body/detail, so a
              constant type like {:turn/error-type :bot/cancelled} is not a finding."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-error-provenance.cljs" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*/"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h, matching its siblings
   :timeout-ms  1200000                  ; 20m -- 9.5x the measured run, sized for load
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine: 126.3s at load 5.75, exit 0,
              `SCANNED\t24088/4460 repo(s)`, 5 findings.
              These four fields were missing when the entry landed the same day, and
              admission is all-or-nothing: `ADMISSION FAILED - refusing to run any
              detector`. The tick's state.edn was last written 14:51, the entry landed
              17:48, and the LaunchAgent interval is 6h -- so the break was found in the
              window before the next fire, by a session registering a different detector
              and discovering none could run. The evidence regex requires the ratio form
              this script prints, so `SCANNED\t0/4460` cannot be recorded clean."}

  {:id       :verify-delegation-surfaces
   :title    "authority decided outside the lattice"
   :why      "ADR-2608180200, 2026-08-19: biscuit becomes the delegation centre and the
              decider stays ONE -- kotoba-lang/authority. authority.scope's docstring
              records what a second answer cost: covers? had been written once per URI
              scheme and one copy stripped a trailing * and called starts-with?, so
              kotoba://graph/alice* covered kotoba://graph/alice-evil. That copy is
              fixed and nothing checks for the next one. Wiring 130 repos by hand is not
              a plan; making the fleet able to answer where the decision is made is."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-delegation-surfaces.cljs" "--check" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- the shape changes slowly
   :timeout-ms  1800000                  ; 30m -- 6x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine: ~5m wall, 3,588 repos with a src/ tree, 1 file
              matched and it folds into the lattice -- zero findings. `--self-test` is
              what makes that zero readable: 5/5 cases, including the historical
              cacao.core/covers? body, which the FIRST version of this scan did not
              match. It reported zero findings then too. A detector that reports zero
              and a detector that cannot see are the same output."}

  {:id       :verify-vendored-copies
   :title    "vendored copies vs the upstream they name"
   :why      "ADR-2608121000, 2026-08-12: six repos vendor pay/* and treasury/core.cljc
              into their own src/ because their CI checks out one repo with no west
              workspace. Each copy's header says `diff against upstream to check drift`
              and nothing performed that diff. Several had drifted for weeks on paths
              that decide whether a payment happened -- one was missing the guard whose
              own fix comment says its absence lets an unconfirmed payment be accepted
              as confirmed. The instruction to check was addressed to a person."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-vendored-copies.cljs" "--check" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- 8x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-12 on this machine: 1m41s wall, 34 vendored files, exit 1 with
              --check, 13 stale (7 of them on pay/x402 and treasury/core -- the payment
              paths). Only `git show`/`git log`/`git rev-list` against already
              checked-out repos; no network."}

  {:id       :verify-valueflows-binding
   :title    "the cloud-itonami Valueflows projections, and the ISIC tables under them"
   :why      "ADR-2608153000, 2026-08-15: the binding that puts 1,398 cloud-itonami
              businesses on the Valueflows plane is a committed projection derived from
              blueprints plus the mirrored UN ISIC tables, and every input is outside
              the repo that holds it. Its first version reported 497 classified
              businesses of which up to 121 had the WRONG industry name, because it
              resolved Rev.5-declared codes against the only mirrored table, which was
              Rev.4 -- 83 of the 361 codes in both tables mean different things (1104 is
              soft drinks in Rev.4 and malt in Rev.5). Nothing would have caught that
              recurring. The Rev.4 half of the mirror is also unpinned and disputed
              against the UN legacy structure file on 33 of 414 titles, which 122
              businesses' industry names currently rest on; if that record and the mirror
              stop agreeing, a human has to decide which vintage is authoritative, and
              silently is the one way it must not happen. 2026-08-15 addition: the
              uchiwake resource projection is checked the same way. It is the first real
              economic content on the plane and 163 of its 165 specifications are
              uchiwake's own :representative rather than :authoritative, so a silent
              regeneration that dropped that label would turn inference into
              measurement."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-valueflows-binding.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}"
              "--findings"]
   :cwd      :repo
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h -- the inputs change when a blueprint or
                                         ; the mirror does, neither of which is hourly
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 200x the measured runtime, because the
                                         ; binder subprocess walks 1,398 blueprints
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-15 on this machine: 3s wall, SCANNED 1398, 0 findings, exit 0. All
              three checks were shown to discriminate before the entry was added, each
              after asserting the mutation had actually applied: a mutated projection
              gives binding-stale, an absent projection exits 2 on the SCANNED floor, a
              one-byte change to the Rev.5 CSV gives rev5-pin-drift, an edited
              class-titles-that-differ gives rev4-conflict-count-mismatch, and a deleted
              PROVENANCE.edn gives rev4-provenance-record-absent. It shells out to
              scripts/itonami-valueflows-bind.cljs --check rather than re-deriving,
              because a second copy of the classification logic is how one copy gets
              fixed and the other does not. Reads only files; no network -- the Rev.4
              comparison is against the RECORD, not a refetch, so a UN outage says
              nothing about this workspace."}

  {:id       :verify-require-graph
   :title    "namespaces migrated to .kotoba with the callers left behind"
   :why      "ADR-2608071000, 2026-07-21: kotoba-lang/dsl-core deleted
              src/kotoba/dsl/problem.cljc for problem.kotoba and updated no consumer.
              kotoba.dsl.problem stopped loading on JVM, nbb and ClojureScript, and
              12 checked-out repos stayed broken for 17 days with nobody noticing --
              src/ execution paths, not just tests. Nobody noticed because every CI
              looked only at its own repo: dsl-core's own tests were green."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-require-graph.cljs" "--root" "{{root}}" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 86400000                 ; 24h -- see :measured; this one is expensive
   :timeout-ms  2700000                  ; 45m -- 5x the measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-12 on this machine: 8m0s under the tick (8m48s standalone; 29% CPU --
              it is dominated by per-repo subprocesses, and this machine runs many
              concurrent agents), 3571 repos with a source root, exit 1: 2 orphan
              namespaces that gate, 97 report-only sibling gaps that do not.
              24h rather than 6h because a .kotoba migration lands on human timescales
              and four idle 9-minute sweeps a day is a real cost on a shared machine."}

  {:id       :verify-source-path-pins
   :title    "cross-repo :source-path checkouts against the revision west.yml pins"
   :why      "A shadow-cljs build whose `:source-path` points at a sibling checkout compiles
              whatever revision that checkout happens to be at, which is not necessarily the
              one manifest/west.yml pins. The artifact is then unreproducible: nothing in it
              records which revision was compiled, and a rebuild on a machine whose checkout
              sits elsewhere produces different output from the same manifest.
              ⚠ **This detector has caught nothing, because nothing has ever run it on a
              schedule** -- ADR-2608196100 measured it as one of twelve verifiers with no
              runner and no twin. What justifies running it is not a past catch but a present
              measurement: 2026-08-19, 240 of 383 cross-repo source-paths resolve to a
              revision west.yml does not pin. The property it guards is violated at scale
              right now, and the registry's NEW/RESOLVED machinery is what turns that standing
              number into a signal when it moves.
              Adjacent and already registered: verify-shadow-source-paths asks whether a
              declared :source-path EXISTS. This asks whether the checkout behind it is the
              pinned revision. A build can pass the first and fail this one."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-source-path-pins.cljs" "{{root}}"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 37x the measured runtime, sized against
                                         ; load, not against the quiet number
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine, twice: 16s wall both times, at load 7.6 and 8.3.
              383 relative cross-repo :source-path entries across 31 shadow-cljs.edn files,
              240 findings, exit 1 -- it has no --report flag, and the tick does not need one
              because `:status` is never :ok on an exit code alone; the evidence regex has to
              match, and this detector prints SCANNED<TAB>383 before any finding.
              `{{root}}` is passed explicitly on purpose: the script's default root is a
              HARDCODED absolute path to this machine's checkout, so a tick running from a
              worktree would silently scan the wrong tree without it."}

  {:id       :verify-layer-deps
   :title    "deps.edn :local/root edges against the layer DAG in manifest/layers.edn"
   :why      "manifest/layers.edn declares a dependency DAG over the workspace's layers and
              this is the only thing that checks it. ADR-2607289600 records the state it is
              guarding against, measured: the layer plane is `定義のみ` -- west.yml has zero
              `layer-*` groups and gen-west-manifest.cljs does not read layers.edn -- so a
              violating :local/root edge is caught by NOTHING else in the pipeline. It has
              also already found real breakage: the Phase-1 dedup worklist
              (90-docs/2607171100-phase1-dedup-worklist.csv, row 210) records
              cloud-itonami-isic-855 with a :local/root one directory too high, credited to
              this script.
              ⚠ 2026-08-13 correction (ADR-2608132600): this line previously said the seven
              standing violations were `numbered directories re-created inside three app repos
              after layers.edn froze the list`. That was wrong, and it is the failure mode this
              registry's `:why` field is supposed to prevent -- a plausible story written from
              the finding text rather than from the repos. All seven directories were added
              between 2026-04-08 and 2026-06-26, BEFORE layers.edn froze the list on 2026-07-17
              (`git log --diff-filter=A`). They reappeared because layers.edn keys its exemption
              lists on WEST PATH, and three GitHub org-transfers and one rename moved the paths
              while the exemptions stayed: gftdcojp/app-aozora -> network-awai/app-aozora
              (gh id 1275051908), gftdcojp/club-shinshi -> network-awai/club-shinshi (1281275923),
              gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp -> gftdcojp/apps-gftdcojp (1136866292). Fixed in
              layers.edn; 7 -> 0. What this detector actually guards against is therefore two
              things, and only the first was written down: a violating :local/root edge, and an
              exemption list whose keys have drifted off the repos they exempt."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath" "{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-layer-deps.cljs" "--report" "--findings"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  900000                   ; 15m -- 7x the worst measured runtime
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-13 on this machine, twice, and the spread is the point. At load ~1
              (10 cores): 26.5s wall. At load 133: 2m06s wall for the same work, 10.1s of it
