  {:id       :verify-kip-quorum
   :title    "the signatures behind every KIP that has actually been admitted"
   :why      "A :final KIP is a decision this workspace has agreed to be bound by, and the
              only thing standing between `:status :final` in a text file and a decision
              nobody signed is this script checking the ed25519 quorum on it. Nothing ran it
              on a schedule: ADR-2608196100 counted it among twelve verifiers with no runner
              and no twin, and a check that never runs cannot notice a signature going bad,
              a signer being removed from the allowed set, or a KIP being flipped to :final
              by hand.
              It reads two west checkouts -- orgs/kotoba-lang/kip (the registry) and
              orgs/kotoba-lang/kagami (did:key resolution) -- so it cannot be a fleet gate:
              fleet ships one repo's tree to a node, and neither of those would be there.
              That is what puts it here rather than in scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath"
              "{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/kip/src:{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/kagami/src:{{repo}}:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-kip-quorum.cljs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "VERIFIED\t[1-9][0-9]*\t"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  120000                   ; 2m -- 240x the measured runtime; the cost here is
                                         ; nbb startup, not the work
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine: 0.2s and 0.5s at load 20.2 for the registered form,
              0.2s twice for --all (which does the ed25519 verification). Three directions
              shown with the same script, all measured, not argued:
                --kips <tampered>  one hex digit flipped in a signature ->
                                   FINDING error quorum-not-met:kip-0001, exit 1
                --all              untampered -> VERIFIED 1, exit 0
                (registered form)  VERIFIED 0, exit 3
              **The third line is why the evidence key is VERIFIED and not SCANNED.** Before
              today this script exited 0 on an empty subject: two KIPs read, none :final, so
              nothing was checked -- and it said so in prose, to a human, while handing every
              machine a zero. SCANNED counts KIPs read (2); VERIFIED counts KIPs whose quorum
              was checked (0), and the registry's qualifier files a run with no evidence line
              as :inconclusive rather than clean. So this detector will read as INCONCLUSIVE
              until the first KIP goes :final, which is the true state of the registry and not
              a fault in the detector. Exit 3 is the 'could not answer' code ADR-2608136000
              asks for -- neither 0 nor 1."}


  {:id       :verify-app-content-address
   :title    "application manifests that identify their document by location, not by content"
   :why      "ADR-2608157000 decided an application's identity is the CID of its document,
              not the path it happens to sit at. 80 of 516 kotoba.app.edn manifests still
              identify by location only -- measured today, not asserted -- and nothing was
              watching whether that number moves in either direction.
              The judge is `content-address.core/addressed?`; this script deliberately carries
              no second copy of that decision. It reads two things under orgs/ (every app
              manifest, and the library that judges them), so it cannot be a fleet gate."
   :argv     ["nbb" "--classpath"
              "{{repo}}:{{root}}/orgs/kotoba-lang/content-address/src:{{repo}}/scripts/nbb_compat"
              "{{repo}}/scripts/verify-app-content-address.cljs"]
   :cwd      :root
   :findings :protocol
   :evidence "SCANNED\t[1-9][0-9]*"
   :interval-ms 21600000                 ; 6h
   :timeout-ms  600000                   ; 10m -- 70x the measured runtime, sized against the
                                         ; load this machine actually runs at, not the quiet number
   :writes   :none
   :measured "2026-08-19 on this machine, four directions:
                fixture: one addressed manifest   -> SCANNED 1,   0 findings, exit 0
                fixture: one located manifest     -> SCANNED 1,   1 finding,  exit 1
                the real orgs/ tree               -> SCANNED 516, 80 findings, exit 1, 8.5s at load 12.9
                library not checked out           -> exit 2 (refusal)
              Two defects were fixed to make this registrable, and both are this loop's
              recurring shape:
              (1) **It reported 80 violations to a human and zero to any machine.** The
                  located-only rows printed as prose (and only under --list); the registry
                  parses FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail and nothing else. SCANNED
                  matched, no FINDING was seen, and the run would have been filed clean.
              (2) **Its refusal exited 1, not 2.** `set! exitCode 2` followed by a throw is
                  overridden by nbb's error handler, so 'could not answer' reached the caller
                  wearing the code for 'found violations'. It now exits without throwing.
              Everything else here was already careful -- three-valued exits, no mirrored
              judge, an explicit refusal when the library is absent, worktree copies excluded
              from the count. The gap was that its output was addressed to a reader.
              No root argument is passed: the script's default is the relative `orgs`, which
              keeps FINDING keys repo-relative and therefore stable across machines."}

